WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:08.433
In this walkthrough we'll explore the
party tree and the holiday calendars
behind every date, in the OpenTRMS Workbench.

2
00:00:08.433 --> 00:00:18.233
Party Hierarchy holds everyone the system knows
about, arranged as a tree. Every trade points at
two of these — one on your side, one on theirs.

3
00:00:18.233 --> 00:00:29.933
The tree has three levels. A group at the top, business
units beneath it, and legal entities at the bottom — and
it is the legal entity that actually trades and settles.

4
00:00:29.933 --> 00:00:38.633
Each node carries a code and a type.
Alpha Group is marked internal, which
means it is part of your own organisation.

5
00:00:38.633 --> 00:00:50.767
Beta Group is external. That single distinction decides a
great deal — which side of a trade an entity can sit on,
and which limits and settlement instructions apply to it.

6
00:00:50.767 --> 00:00:58.133
Collapse all returns the tree to its top level, which
is the quickest way back when the hierarchy is large.

7
00:00:58.133 --> 00:01:05.767
Calendars are the other piece of
reference data you meet early. The
Workbench keeps one per financial centre.

8
00:01:05.767 --> 00:01:15.633
London, TARGET for the euro, and New York
each hold their own holidays, and the count
beside each one tells you how well populated it is.

9
00:01:15.633 --> 00:01:23.133
Selecting a calendar lists its holidays. These
are the dates the system will refuse to settle on.

10
00:01:23.133 --> 00:01:35.567
This is why a spot trade booked on a Thursday might settle the
following Monday, and why the same trade in two currencies can
settle on different days. The calendar decides, not the count of days.

11
00:01:35.567 --> 00:01:43.667
That completes Parties and Calendars. Every
surface shown here can also be opened from the
agent panel on the right, by asking for it in plain English.
